{"name":"Canter Peach","hex":"#f6d3bb","fetched":"2026-10-12","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"canter","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["A gait of a horse between a trot and a gallop, consisting of three beats and a \"suspension\" phase, where there are no feet on the ground. Also describing this gait on other four-legged animals.","A ride on a horse at such speed."]},{"part":"Verb","definitions":["To move at such pace.","To cause to move at a canter; to ride (a horse) at a canter."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/canter"},{"term":"peach","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["Any tree of species Prunus persica, native to China and now widely cultivated throughout temperate regions, having pink flowers and edible fruit.","Soft juicy stone fruit of the peach tree, having yellow flesh, downy, red-tinted yellow skin, and a deeply sculptured pit or stone containing a single seed."]},{"part":"Adjective","definitions":["Of or pertaining to the color peach.","Particularly pleasing or agreeable."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/peach"}],"entries":[{"title":"Canter and gallop","blocks":[{"text":"The canter and gallop are variations on the fastest gait that can be performed by a horse or other equine. The canter is a controlled three-beat gait, while the gallop is a faster, four-beat variation of the same gait. It is a natural gait possessed by all horses, faster than most horses' trot, or ambling gaits. The gallop is the fastest gait of the horse, averaging about 40 to 48 kilometres per hour. The speed of the canter varies between 16 and 27 kilometres per hour depending on the length of the horse's stride. A variation of the canter, seen in western riding, is called a lope, and is generally quite slow, no more than 13–19 kilometres per hour (8–12 mph)."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/1/12/Aveger_show06-1-.JPG/330px-Aveger_show06-1-.JPG?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Canter_and_gallop","wikidata":"Q1007807"},{"title":"Peach","blocks":[{"text":"The peach is a deciduous tree that bears edible juicy fruits with various characteristics. Most are simply called peaches, while the glossy-skinned, non-fuzzy varieties are called nectarines. Though from the same species, they are regarded commercially as different fruits."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/9/92/Illustration_Prunus_persica_clean_no_descr.jpg/330px-Illustration_Prunus_persica_clean_no_descr.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Peach","wikidata":"Q13189"},{"title":"Peach (color)","blocks":[{"text":"Peach is a color that is named for the pale color of the interior flesh of the peach fruit. Like the color apricot, the color peach is paler than most actual peach fruits and seems to have been formulated primarily to create a pastel palette of colors for interior design."}],"link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Peach_(color)","wikidata":"Q5148721"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/e/e8/Miniature_Horse_Runs_Through_the_Pasture.jpg/500px-Miniature_Horse_Runs_Through_the_Pasture.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/e/e8/Miniature_Horse_Runs_Through_the_Pasture.jpg/1280px-Miniature_Horse_Runs_Through_the_Pasture.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/e/e8/Miniature_Horse_Runs_Through_the_Pasture.jpg/500px-Miniature_Horse_Runs_Through_the_Pasture.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"A miniature horse at a gallop","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AMiniature_Horse_Runs_Through_the_Pasture.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/t